<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Much Is the Pineapple?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>Reading: reading1: Write short responses to questions about a text.
            reading2: Outline key information in simple factual texts on familiar subjects.
            phonemicAwareness: Blend spoken simple onsets and rhymes to form real words (onset /c/ and rhyme /at/).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>Reading: Can write short responses to questions about items and prices (e.g., 'How many apples?' 'Five apples, please.').
            Can read and outline key information about prices and quantities in texts about the market or groceries (e.g., 'Four bananas cost one dollar.').
            Can blend simple spoken onsets and rhymes to form familiar words (e.g., /t/ and /ɛn/ to form the word 'ten').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of market items (pineapple, potatoes, cassava, apples, bananas) and their corresponding prices (e.g., "Pineapple - $3"). Students call out the name of the item and its price. The teacher asks "How much is the pineapple?" and students respond by reading the price. Teacher also shows a simple "shopping list" with items and quantities (e.g., "3 apples, 1 banana").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to read a simple shopping list aloud, pointing to each item and its quantity. For instance, "I need three apples and one banana." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /p/ in 'pineapple' and /m/ in 'money'. The teacher also models blending simple onsets and rhymes by orally stating /t/ and /ɛn/ and then blending them to form the word 'ten'. Students repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a shopping list or a story?" (Shopping list)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'pineapple' cost money?" (Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word is /t/ /ɛn/?" (Ten)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple factual text in the format of a "Market Price List" or a short "Grocery Store Flyer" displaying various items (pineapple, potatoes, cassava, apples, bananas) with their prices and quantities (e.g., "Pineapple: $3 each," "Potatoes: $2 per bag," "Bananas: $1 for 4"). The text also includes a few simple sentences like "The market has fresh fruits and vegetables." or "We need to buy three items today."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding key information in simple factual texts about familiar subjects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students quickly read the text and answer: "What is this text mostly about?" (The market prices for food).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Reading:</b> The teacher provides True/False statements based on the text (e.g., "True or False: A pineapple costs five dollars." "True or False: Bananas are cheap."). Students read the text carefully to determine if the statements are true or false.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match pictures of items to their prices as listed in the text.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> WH-Questions ('How much is it?', 'How many?'), Quantities ('I need five potatoes.'), Present Simple ('I want three apples and one banana.'). Vocabulary: market, price, shopping list, item, pineapple, cashier, money, cassava, potatoes, buy, need, want, cost, cheap, expensive, numbers 1-100.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Read & Respond):</b> Students receive a worksheet with the "Market Price List" from Stage 2. Below the text, there are simple WH-questions (e.g., "How much is one pineapple?", "How many bananas cost one dollar?", "What is the price of a bag of potatoes?"). Students read the text and write short responses to the questions (e.g., "$3", "Four bananas", "$2").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Outline & Identify):</b> Students are given a new, slightly different "Shopping List" with items and quantities, but with prices missing. They are also given a separate "Price Sheet" for a different market. Students must read both documents to find the prices for the items on their shopping list and write them next to each item (e.g., "Apples: 5 – Price: $4"). This activity requires them to outline key information by cross-referencing two simple factual texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check (Read and Verify):</b> In pairs, students exchange their completed "Shopping List with Prices" from the previous activity. Each student reads their partner's list and verifies the prices against the original "Price Sheet." They politely correct any discrepancies (e.g., "Excuse me, I think the potatoes cost three dollars, not two."). This encourages careful reading and cross-referencing.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Reading Comprehension with a written product.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students receive a "Customer's Note" from a person who wants to buy groceries at the market. This note is a short, simple text that includes what the customer wants to buy and some preferences (e.g., "Dear Market, I need fresh fruit. I want two pineapples and five apples. How much is the cassava? I have ten dollars. Thank you!"). Students read the note carefully. After reading, they answer specific comprehension questions about the note on a separate sheet (e.g., "How many pineapples does the customer want?", "How many apples does the customer want?", "Does the customer ask about the price of cassava?"). The questions specifically target their ability to extract quantities and price inquiries from a simple text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in reading simple factual texts, collaborative interaction (if students work in pairs to discuss answers first), and communicative task completion by extracting and writing key information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during pair work in Practice stage and written work in Production stage. Quick check of short responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students read a short "Market Specials" flyer with 3-4 items and their prices/quantities. They then answer two or three short-response questions about the flyer (e.g., "How much does one pineapple cost?", "How many potatoes can I buy for one dollar?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of written responses to reading comprehension questions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify key information (items, prices, quantities) from a simple text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of short written answers.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read a shopping list and find the prices." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write answers about how much things cost." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul style="padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me today when reading about market prices?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about shopping was difficult for me?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What reading strategies worked best for 4th graders to understand prices and quantities? Were the texts at the appropriate CEFR A1.2 level? What adjustments are needed to support students with outlining key information or writing short responses for the next lesson? Did the phonemic awareness activity reinforce number words effectively?</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson, we will use the vocabulary and understanding of prices we gained today to practice speaking and interacting in a market scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>